            <ArticleTitle>A case of eosinophilic gastroenteritis with severe peripheral eosinophilia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>331-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Eosinophilic gastroenteritis is a rare heterogeneous disorder of undetermined etiology that is characterized by eosinophilic infiltration of the gastrointestinal tissues and various clinical manifestations. We report an uncommon case of eosinophilic gastroenteritis involving a patient with a short history of mild upper abdominal pain, severe peripheral eosinophilia (absolute eosinophil count of &gt; 5,000 cells per microL), and ascites. The patient was treated successfully with a course of methylprednisolone.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
